# 6. Literature Analysis

## 6.1 Paper 1 – Anand, Singh and Saurabh (2021)

### Purpose

The paper investigates the use of artificial intelligence in healthcare and examines how AI and medical practices can work together.

### Methodology

The authors use a systematic review approach to examine existing research on AI and healthcare.

### Key Findings

The study identifies several potential applications of AI in healthcare, including diagnosis, prediction, data analysis and clinical decision support.

### Contribution to Research Question

This paper provides background evidence that AI can improve healthcare delivery by supporting healthcare professionals and processing large amounts of medical information.

### Research Gap

More research is required to determine how AI systems perform in real-world clinical environments and whether they improve patient outcomes.

---

## 6.2 Paper 2 – Shaban-Nejad et al. (2021)

### Purpose

The paper examines the importance of explainable AI in healthcare.

### Methodology

This paper provides a conceptual discussion of explainable and trustworthy AI.

### Key Findings

The authors emphasise four important principles:

- Fairness
- Accountability
- Transparency
- Trust

### Contribution to Research Question

The paper demonstrates that AI accuracy alone is not sufficient. Healthcare professionals need to understand and trust AI-generated recommendations.

### Research Gap

More empirical research is required to determine which explainability techniques are most useful to clinicians and patients.

---

## 6.3 Paper 3 – Moreno-Sánchez (2023)

### Purpose

The study investigates an explainable AI model for the early diagnosis of chronic kidney disease.

### Methodology

The researchers develop and evaluate a machine-learning model using healthcare data and incorporate explainability into the model.

### Key Findings

The study demonstrates the potential of AI to support early disease diagnosis while providing explanations for predictions.

### Contribution to Research Question

The paper provides evidence that AI can improve early diagnosis and clinical decision support.

### Research Gap

Further validation using different hospitals, datasets and patient populations is required.

---

## 6.4 Paper 4 – Patra et al. (2023)

### Purpose

The paper reviews recent developments and applications of deep learning in healthcare.

### Methodology

A review of existing deep-learning research was conducted.

### Key Findings

Deep learning can be used for medical diagnosis and treatment. However, challenges include:

- Data quality
- Privacy
- Interpretability
- Ethics
- Regulation

### Contribution to Research Question

This paper demonstrates both the benefits and challenges of AI-based healthcare systems.

### Research Gap

More research is needed to move deep-learning models from experimental datasets into real clinical environments.

---

## 6.5 Paper 5 – Chakraborty et al. (2023)

### Purpose

The paper investigates an explainable AI system for identifying the onset of sepsis.

### Methodology

The researchers develop a machine-learning clinical assistance model and incorporate explainability.

### Key Findings

AI can potentially identify patients at risk of sepsis earlier and assist clinicians with decision-making.

### Contribution to Research Question

The study provides a practical example of AI supporting early clinical intervention.

### Research Gap

The effectiveness of the system needs to be evaluated in real clinical environments and through its impact on patient outcomes.

---

## 6.6 Paper 6 – Abououf et al. (2024)

### Purpose

The study investigates explainable AI for detecting events and anomalies in healthcare monitoring systems.

### Methodology

The researchers use an autoencoder-based approach and KernelSHAP for explainability.

### Key Findings

AI can identify abnormal healthcare events, while explainability can help users understand the model's predictions.

### Contribution to Research Question

This paper demonstrates the potential of AI for continuous patient monitoring and early identification of abnormal conditions.

### Research Gap

Further research should evaluate the system using real-time clinical data and determine whether clinicians find the explanations useful.

---

## 6.7 Paper 7 – Moghadam et al. (2024)

### Purpose

The paper examines the use of AI in nursing and geriatric care for chronic diseases.

### Methodology

The authors conduct a systematic literature review.

### Key Findings

AI can support nursing through patient monitoring, prediction and chronic disease management.

### Contribution to Research Question

This paper demonstrates that AI is not limited to medical diagnosis and can also support nursing and patient care.

### Research Gap

More research is needed into usability, clinical workflow integration and the effect of AI on nursing practice and patient outcomes.

---

## 6.8 Paper 8 – Moudoud, Abou El Houda and Brik (2025)

### Purpose

The paper investigates privacy and fairness in healthcare AI.

### Methodology

The researchers propose a framework combining federated learning, edge computing, blockchain, secure multiparty computation and fairness-aware learning.

### Key Findings

Privacy-preserving and fairness-aware approaches can potentially allow healthcare organisations to collaborate on AI without centrally sharing all sensitive patient information.

### Contribution to Research Question

This paper directly addresses the privacy and fairness challenges associated with healthcare AI.

### Research Gap

The computational cost, scalability and real-world deployment of these technologies require further investigation.

---

## 6.9 Paper 9 – Bose, Pal and Maity (2025)

### Purpose

The paper investigates interpretable AI models for breast cancer risk and detection.

### Methodology

The authors conduct a scoping review of existing research.

### Key Findings

Explainable AI is increasingly being used in breast cancer research. SHAP is one of the commonly used explainability techniques.

### Contribution to Research Question

The study demonstrates how interpretability can increase transparency in high-risk healthcare decisions.

### Research Gap

Future studies should determine whether AI explanations actually improve clinician decision-making and patient trust.

---

## 6.10 Paper 10 – Marengo and Santamato (2025)

### Purpose

The study investigates foundation models in healthcare and examines technical and governance issues.

### Methodology

The authors use systematic-review methods together with literature mining and topic-modelling techniques.

### Key Findings

The research identifies significant interest in technical performance, safety, bias and transparency. However, accountability and patient-centred considerations receive comparatively less attention.

### Contribution to Research Question

The study demonstrates that emerging healthcare AI requires governance and accountability in addition to technical performance.

### Research Gap

Further research is needed into:

- AI safety
- Hallucinations
- Bias
- Privacy
- Accountability
- Regulation
- Patient-centred AI

